## Statement

If $H$ is an irreducible positive-energy unitary module as in [[def-positive-energy-unitary-affine-module]], then its ground space $H_0$ is an irreducible module for the compact zero-mode algebra $\mathfrak k$.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The grading, mode adjoints, finite-dimensional energy spaces, and irreducibility in the definition.

[F1] [[def-positive-energy-unitary-affine-module]]: $H_0$ is finite-dimensional and nonzero, zero modes preserve $H_0$, and $X(0)^*=-X(0)$ for $X\in\mathfrak k$.

[F2] [[def-untwisted-affine-central-extension]]: the commutator of two current modes is one current mode plus a scalar central term.

## Proof

1.1 Among the nonzero $\mathfrak k$-invariant subspaces of finite-dimensional $H_0$, choose one, $V$, of least positive dimension. Its orthogonal complement is invariant: if $w\perp V$ and $v\in V$, then $\langle X(0)w,v\rangle=-\langle w,X(0)v\rangle=0$. Thus $V$ is an irreducible $\mathfrak k$-module. [F1, choose]

2.1 Every positive current mode annihilates $V$, since it lowers the nonnegative energy below zero; zero modes preserve $V$. Any finite word in current modes can be reordered into negative modes, then zero modes, then positive modes by repeatedly using $AB=BA+[A,B]$. Each commutator term has shorter word length by [F2], so induction on word length and the finite number of inversions gives a finite reordering. Positive modes then kill $V$, and negative modes raise energy. Thus the current submodule generated by $V$ has ground space exactly $V$. It is nonzero, so irreducibility of $H$ makes it all of $H$; hence $H_0=V$. [F1, F2, step 1.1, construct, algebra] ∎
